<TopicPage slug="subjects/neuroscience/intermediate/movement-and-the-motor-system">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Movement looks simple and is the brain's hardest computation: <strong>converting intention
    into the exact contraction of hundreds of muscles in millisecond timing</strong>. The motor
    system is a hierarchy of specialised controllers — and each level's failure produces a
    different, diagnosable signature.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the motor hierarchy and what each level contributes" widget="equationStep" config={motorCfg} />

  <h2>How movement gets learned</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>From conscious to automatic:</strong> new skills run through the cortex (clumsy, effortful); practice shifts them to cerebellum and basal ganglia (smooth, effortless). "Muscle memory" is neural — the muscles learned nothing.</li>
    <li><strong>Mirror neurons (discovered 1996, macaques):</strong> fire both when acting and watching the same action — possibly relevant to imitation and empathy; how far the claim extends is actively debated.</li>
    <li><strong>The body map is malleable:</strong> the cortical hand area enlarges in musicians; amputees feel phantom limbs in cortical real estate that got reassigned. The map is use-dependent.</li>
    <li><strong>Motor neuroscience meets engineering:</strong> brain–computer interfaces (degraded movement restored via implanted electrodes — Neuralink, BrainGate) read motor cortex signals directly. The movement lesson is where neurology and the AI ladder shake hands.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kandel et al., Principles of Neural Science; Shadmehr & Wise, The Computational Neurobiology of Reaching and Pointing; Rizzolatti on mirror neurons.</p>
</TopicPage>
